                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Inmates is a short psychological horror adventure built around confinement, fragmented memories, and the unsettling feeling that reality cannot entirely be trusted. Developed by Davit Andreasyan and published by Iceberg Interactive, it puts players in the role of Jonathan, who wakes inside a deteriorating prison without understanding how he arrived there. His surroundings feel strangely familiar, and a mysterious voice communicating through a radio only raises more questions. Rather than immediately explaining the situation, the game slowly reveals pieces of Jonathan's past while encouraging the player to question whether the prison is a physical place, a nightmare, or something more personal.\n\nThe prison itself carries much of the experience. Narrow corridors, gloomy cells, abandoned rooms, damaged walls, and carefully controlled lighting create an environment that constantly feels oppressive. Inmates rarely needs enormous environments to establish tension because its confined architecture naturally creates a sense of isolation. Darkness and shadows make seemingly ordinary rooms uncomfortable to explore, while environmental sounds help maintain the impression that something may be waiting nearby. The atmosphere is generally more important than direct confrontation, making this a horror game that tries to unsettle the player psychologically rather than overwhelm them with continuous action.\n\nGameplay primarily consists of first-person exploration, interacting with objects, searching for clues, and solving puzzles. There is considerably less emphasis on combat or complicated survival mechanics, so players expecting weapons, inventory management, or frequent battles should adjust their expectations. Progress instead depends on investigating the environment and discovering how different pieces of Jonathan's situation connect. The slower pace suits the story because it gives players time to absorb the disturbing surroundings rather than rushing from one encounter to another.\n\nPuzzles provide the main mechanical interruption to exploration. They encourage observation and give players reasons to examine rooms carefully instead of simply following corridors toward the next story sequence. While the puzzles do not transform the game into an especially complex puzzle adventure, they complement its mystery-focused structure. The best moments occur when environmental details, puzzle solving, and narrative information work together, making progress feel like uncovering another layer of Jonathan's troubled memories.\n\nThe psychological horror elements are ultimately more significant than traditional scares. Jonathan's perception is unreliable, and the game repeatedly plays with dreams, memories, disturbing imagery, and unexplained events. The prison becomes more than a convenient horror setting because its familiarity to Jonathan suggests a connection to his past. This gives the story a personal dimension and creates curiosity about why he is there. The mystery of escape gradually becomes intertwined with understanding the protagonist himself, giving the otherwise straightforward exploration some additional narrative weight.\n\nOne of the game's most noticeable limitations is its modest scope. A typical playthrough lasts only a few hours, leaving relatively little room for its concepts to become substantially more elaborate. The basic exploration mechanics can also feel simplistic, especially for players accustomed to horror games with deeper stealth, combat, or survival systems. Long stretches of walking and searching may feel slow when the atmosphere is not enough to maintain the player's attention. Replay value is similarly limited once the central mystery and puzzle solutions are known.\n\nIts short duration can nevertheless work in its favor. Inmates delivers its story without stretching the experience far beyond what its mechanics can comfortably support. The limited length makes it suitable for players wanting a psychological horror game that can be completed in one or two sessions. Achievements and other Steam features provide a little additional incentive for completionists, although the narrative and atmosphere remain the primary reasons to play.\n\nSteam Deck compatibility is currently best considered unverified rather than officially supported. The game was originally designed around Windows PC requirements, and the absence of an official Steam Deck compatibility rating means portable players should not assume completely trouble-free operation. Controller support may make handheld play more practical, but compatibility, performance, text readability, or other behavior can potentially require manual adjustments.\n\nInmates succeeds most effectively as a compact atmospheric horror story rather than a deep survival horror game. Its decaying prison is suitably uncomfortable, the mystery surrounding Jonathan provides motivation to continue exploring, and the combination of environmental storytelling and puzzles gives the experience more purpose than a simple haunted-house walkthrough. Its limited mechanical complexity and short duration prevent it from reaching the depth of larger psychological horror adventures, but those same qualities also make it approachable for anyone looking for a concise and unsettling experience. For players who enjoy slow exploration, disturbing environments, psychological themes, and mysteries built around an unreliable perception of reality, it offers an intriguing journey through a place where escaping physically may only be part of the problem.\n\nRating: 7/10",
